----- stdout -----
monochange discovers packages across Cargo, npm/pnpm/Bun, Deno, Dart/Flutter, Python, and Go, then coordinates version bumps, changelogs, and release automation from a single monochange.toml config.

Usage: monochange [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Release Commands:
  next           Show the next version for each release group and package without writing files
  create         Create a changeset file for one or more packages
  preview        Preview planned version bumps, changelogs, and release artifacts without writing files
  prepare        Prepare version bumps, changelogs, and release artifacts
  publish        Publish packages and inspect registry publishing state
  affected       Evaluate affected packages and changeset coverage
  diagnose       Inspect changeset provenance and review metadata

Workspace Commands:
  init           Generate monochange.toml with detected packages and groups
  populate       Check for default CLI workflow aliases (none are provided in this version)
  discover       Discover packages across supported ecosystems
  config         Render resolved monochange configuration and workspace metadata
  check          Validate configuration, changesets, and run manifest lint rules
  versions       List package and group versions or sync internal dependency constraints
  lint           Inspect and scaffold manifest lint rules
  migrate        Audit or migrate release metadata for monochange repositories

Change Analysis Commands:
  change         Determine the release severity of repository changes
  changeset      Inspect and validate pending changeset intent
  api            Inspect lower-level API diffs
  analyze        Analyze semantic changes for one package across main, head, and optional release baselines
  notes          Render one configured release-note output without modifying release files

Automation Commands:
  run            Run workflow commands defined in monochange.toml
  step           Run built-in release workflow steps
  command        Open an interactive dashboard for adding or editing config-defined CLI commands
  mcp            Start the monochange MCP (Model Context Protocol) server over stdin/stdout
  skill          Read or install the bundled monochange agent skill
  subagents      Generate repo-local monochange subagents and agent guidance files
  snapshot       Print a normalized JSON snapshot of the CLI surface
  help           Show detailed help for a command

Workflow Commands (monochange.toml):
  change         Create a change file for one or more packages (run it with `monochange run change`)
  publish-check  Validate the release and preview package publishing in dry-run mode
  release        Prepare a pull request release from discovered change files. It should only be run in a github action.

Options:
  -q, --quiet
          Suppress result, progress, and diagnostic output without changing execution

  -v, --verbose
          Show full result lists and logs, every step timing, and progress notes

      --progress-format <FORMAT>
          Control progress output on stderr
          
          [possible values: auto, unicode, ascii, json]

      --jq <EXPRESSION>
          Filter explicit JSON output; requires `--format json` or `--format json-min`

      --snapshot
          Print a normalized JSON snapshot for this command subtree

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Quick Start

1. Create or update monochange.toml for your workspace:

  monochange init

2. Validate configuration and changeset targets before making release changes:

  monochange step validate

3. Inspect detected package ids and groups when authoring changesets or workflow inputs:

  monochange discover --format json

4. Check what the next version will be before preparing a release. This reads pending changesets and writes nothing:

  monochange next
  monochange next --format json

5. Run repository-defined workflows as monochange <command> (or monochange run <command>, which also works when a built-in command shares the name), or call immutable built-in steps directly with monochange step <name>.

6. Preview before mutating files, publishing packages, creating tags, or opening release requests:

  monochange run release --dry-run --diff
  monochange preview --diff

Run monochange help <command> or monochange help step <name> for command-specific options.


